      <!-- TEXTURE DETAIL -->
      <div class="term-detail texture" id="detail-texture">
        <div class="term-detail-header">
          <div class="term-detail-title">TEXTURE</div>
          <button class="close-btn" onclick="closeTerm('texture')">✕ CLOSE</button>
        </div>

        <div class="definition-box">
          Texture is the pattern inside a tile. A flat tile filled with one color reads as a painted surface. The same tile with mortar lines, cracked edges, or varied pixels reads as stone. The pixel pattern carries the material — and the material is part of the story.
        </div>

        <!-- STONE EXAMPLE: two-panel -->
        <div class="scale-example-wrap">
          <div class="gallery-label" style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">Stone — flat vs. textured</div>
          <div class="scale-panels">
            <div class="scale-panel">
              <div class="gallery-label" style="font-size:0.7rem">One tile, repeated</div>
              <div class="scale-scene-wrap">
                <canvas id="tx-flat" width="32" height="32" style="width:192px;height:192px"></canvas>
              </div>
              <div class="scale-panel-desc">Solid fill, tiled 4×4. Every cell is identical. The wall has no weight, no age, no material.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="scale-panel">
              <div class="gallery-label" style="font-size:0.7rem">Two tile variants, alternated</div>
              <div class="scale-scene-wrap">
                <canvas id="tx-textured" width="32" height="32" style="width:192px;height:192px"></canvas>
              </div>
              <div class="scale-panel-desc">Two stone tiles with mortar lines and varied pixel weights, placed in a checkerboard. The repeat breaks — the wall reads as masonry.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="scale-notes">
            <div class="scale-note-col">
              <div class="gallery-label" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem">What makes the difference</div>
              <div class="sc-note-item"><div class="sc-dot" style="background:#8a7a6a"></div><div class="sc-note-txt"><strong>Mortar lines.</strong> Dark pixels at tile edges suggest the gaps between stones. The grid becomes the material.</div></div>
              <div class="sc-note-item"><div class="sc-dot" style="background:#b0a090"></div><div class="sc-note-txt"><strong>Pixel weight variation.</strong> Lighter and darker pixels within the tile suggest uneven surface. Flat fills have none of this.</div></div>
              <div class="sc-note-item"><div class="sc-dot" style="background:#6a5a4a"></div><div class="sc-note-txt"><strong>Two variants, not one.</strong> A single textured tile repeated still reads as wallpaper. The second variant breaks the pattern.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="scale-note-col">
              <div class="gallery-label" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem">The Bitsy constraint</div>
              <div class="sc-note-txt" style="line-height:1.7">You only have three colors per room. Texture is how you make those three colors do more work.<br><br>A flat stone wall uses one color for the whole tile. A textured stone wall uses all three — background for mortar gaps, tile color for the stone face, highlight for edges catching light.<br><br>Same palette. Different arrangement. Different material.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- INTERACTIVE WIDGET -->
        <div class="gallery-label" style="margin:1.25rem 0 0.75rem">Try it out — draw your own tile</div>

        <div class="tx-widget">
